# .trivyignore — suppressions for the Container Scan (Trivy) CI job (.github/workflows/ci.yml)
#
# Wired into the BLOCKING app-layer scan step only ("Scan image with Trivy — app
# layer (gate)", vuln-type: library). Base-OS CVEs are handled by a separate
# report-only step (vuln-type: os, exit-code 0) and are NOT listed here — see
# docs/trivy-suppressions.md for why the OS layer does not gate.
# This file therefore only ever needs entries for app/library (lang-pkg) findings.
#
# Each entry MUST carry a justification and a re-review date. Remove an entry the
# moment the underlying condition changes (e.g. the base image ships a patched
# assembly) — a fresh `docker build` re-pulls the rolling base tag on every main
# push, so a genuinely-fixed vuln stops being reported without any change here.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MessagePack (MessagePack-CSharp) 2.5.192 — June-2026 advisory batch
#   Fixed upstream in: 2.5.301 (v2 line) / 3.1.7 (v3 line)
#   Re-review by:      2026-10-01  (or sooner if the base image is bumped)

#
# Why this is ignored rather than bumped:
#   MessagePack.dll is a .NET assembly vendored inside the Azure Functions host
#   runtime that ships in our stage-2 base image
#   (mcr.microsoft.com/azure-functions/node:4-node20). It is NOT an npm
#   dependency of this app (grep the package-lock: there is no messagepack), and
#   it is NOT part of our application code under /home/site/wwwroot — it belongs
#   to the Microsoft-maintained host. We do not build the host and cannot bump
#   the assembly independently. `4-node20` is a rolling tag and CI rebuilds fresh
#   on every main push, yet the image still ships 2.5.192, so Microsoft has not
#   yet published a host image carrying MessagePack >= 2.5.301. The scan job
#   already runs with `ignore-unfixed: true`; these advisories surface only
#   because an upstream fix version exists that the base image has not adopted.
#
# Why it is not reachable in our deployment (CWE-20, remote DoS):
#   The vulnerable path is LZ4 decompression / deserialization of *untrusted*
#   MessagePack payloads. This is a Node.js/TypeScript Azure Functions app; it
#   neither references nor invokes MessagePack. The host uses MessagePack only
#   for internal, trusted, in-process communication with its language workers —
#   it does not deserialize attacker-controlled MessagePack from the public HTTP
#   surface. The precondition (an attacker delivering a crafted MessagePack LZ4
#   payload to a deserializer) is therefore not met.
#
# Trivy currently flags CVE-2026-48109 (CVSS 8.2) and CVE-2026-48506 (CVSS 7.5)
# as HIGH on this assembly. The remaining IDs below are the rest of the same
# June-2026 MessagePack-CSharp batch — identical root cause, threat model, and
# fix version — listed so main does not re-redden if Trivy's DB reclassifies any
# of them to HIGH before the base image is patched.
CVE-2026-48109
CVE-2026-48506
CVE-2026-48502
CVE-2026-48510
CVE-2026-48512
CVE-2026-48515
